<!-- hql reference: std/core -->
# `std/core`

```hql
module core
```

The types every other module is written in: the scalars, the collection
types, the open tree `Data`, absence, failure, value order, the format
category and the core traits. A type sits here when a module below it
would otherwise import a module above it: `Data` is a tree of sequences
and maps, so the collection types are core types, and the operations
over them are the `collections` module's.

- [`Orderable`](types/Orderable.hmd) — A type whose values have a total order: of any two values of the type, one comes first or the two are equal.
- [`Scalar`](types/Scalar.hmd) — A leaf: a value with no parts of its own.
- [`Bool`](types/Bool.hmd) — The truth values `true` and `false`: a `Scalar` with equality and no order.
- [`Int`](types/Int.hmd) — A 64-bit whole number, ordered by value: `7 / 2` is `3`, and `7 % 2` is `1`.
- [`Float`](types/Float.hmd) — A 64-bit binary floating-point number, written with the suffix `f` (`2.5f`), ordered by value.
- [`Decimal`](types/Decimal.hmd) — An exact decimal number, written with a decimal point and no suffix (`2.50`), ordered by value: `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` is `true`.
- [`String`](types/String.hmd) — Text, ordered character by character: `"ab" + "cd"` is `"abcd"`, and `"hello"[1]` is `"e"`.
- [`Date`](types/Date.hmd) — A proleptic Gregorian calendar date, ordered by time: `Date("2026-10-24")`.
- [`DateTime`](types/DateTime.hmd) — A date and a time of day with no zone or offset, ordered by time: `DateTime("2026-10-24T09:30:00")`.
- [`Instant`](types/Instant.hmd) — A point on the UTC timeline with nanosecond precision, ordered by time: `Instant("2026-10-24T07:30:00Z")`.
- [`Duration`](types/Duration.hmd) — A signed length of time in nanoseconds, ordered by length: `Duration("PT1H30M")`.
- [`Bytes`](types/Bytes.hmd) — A finite sequence of octets, ordered octet by octet as unsigned numbers, a shorter prefix first (HQL-0053 part 1).
- [`Iterable`](types/Iterable.hmd) — A value whose elements can each be visited: the bound an operation states when it only visits each value.
- [`Collection`](types/Collection.hmd) — A finite multiset of occurrences, unordered, with no runtime representation.
- [`Stream`](types/Stream.hmd) — An ordered, possibly unending run of values `x0, x1, x2, ...`.
- [`Seq`](types/Seq.hmd) — A finite stream: a collection in which every occurrence has exactly one position in `0 .. size(c) - 1`.
- [`List`](types/List.hmd) — A sequence of materialized occurrences; repeated traversal preserves positions.
- [`Set`](types/Set.hmd) — Materialized distinct values under HQL equality: `count(s, x) <= 1`.
- [`Map`](types/Map.hmd) — Materialized associations from distinct keys of `K` to values of `V`; not a `Collection`.
- [`OrderedMap`](types/OrderedMap.hmd) — A `Map` whose keys keep their construction positions: its keys are a `Seq<K>`.
- [`SortedMap`](types/SortedMap.hmd) — An `OrderedMap` whose key positions follow the intrinsic total order of `K`.
- [`Data`](types/Data.hmd) — An open tree whose keys belong to whoever wrote them: what a YAML or JSON document is.
- [`TypeContract`](types/TypeContract.hmd) — The supertype of a closed contract: a structural type declared `type C : TypeContract = {…}` has exactly its declared fields, and is validated as a contract.
- [`DataContract`](types/DataContract.hmd) — The supertype of an open contract: a structural type declared `type D : DataContract = {…}` is a view over `Data` with at least its declared fields, and is validated as a contract.
- [`Option`](types/Option.hmd) — An optional value of `T`: present, holding one `T`, or absent.
- [`Result`](types/Result.hmd) — A computation that may have failed: `ok(value)` with a `T`, or `err(error)` with an `E`.
- [`Ordering`](types/Ordering.hmd) — The result of `compare(a, b)`: `Less`, `Equal` or `Greater`.
- [`Window`](types/Window.hmd) — One row's place in its ordered partition under `over`: the rows with its key, in the order `over` gives them, and its position among them.
- [`Type`](types/Type.hmd) — A type written as an argument: `is(Card)`, `of_type(Card)`.
- [`Format`](types/Format.hmd) — The category of formats: how bytes are documents.
- [`Is`](types/Is.hmd) — What a predicate that tests its parameter's type, `x => x | is(U)`, gives: a `Bool` that, where true, holds the parameter at `U`.
- [`Eq`](types/Eq.hmd) — Equality: `a == b` and `a != b` over a type implementing `Eq` call its `eq`.
- [`Ord`](types/Ord.hmd) — Order: `a < b` and the other comparisons over a type implementing `Ord` call its `compare`.
- [`Add`](types/Add.hmd) — Addition: `a + b` over a type implementing `Add` is `a.add(b)`.
- [`Sub`](types/Sub.hmd) — Subtraction: `a - b` over a type implementing `Sub` is `a.sub(b)`.
- [`Mul`](types/Mul.hmd) — Multiplication: `a * b` over a type implementing `Mul` is `a.mul(b)`.
- [`Div`](types/Div.hmd) — Division: `a / b` over a type implementing `Div` is `a.div(b)`.
- [`Rem`](types/Rem.hmd) — Remainder: `a % b` over a type implementing `Rem` is `a.rem(b)`.
- [`Range`](types/Range.hmd) — A window of positions, what a slice takes: `xs[a:b]` is `xs[Range(start = a, end = b)]`, and a bound left out is absent.
- [`Index`](types/Index.hmd) — A position: `xs[i]` over a type implementing `Index` is `xs.at(i)`, the element there or its absence.
- [`Slice`](types/Slice.hmd) — A part: `xs[a:b]` over a type implementing `Slice` is `xs.slice(Range(start = a, end = b))`, a value of the type itself.
- [`Validator`](types/Validator.hmd) — A check a value passes: a trait extending `Validator` is a validator trait.
- [`Operation`](types/Operation.hmd) — A function from `I` to `O` that also carries its shape: what an executor may assume about splitting the step.
- [`Elementwise`](types/Elementwise.hmd) — An `Operation` whose result for each element depends on that element alone, so it may run over any split of its input: `map` and `filter` are elementwise.
- [`Partition`](types/Partition.hmd) — An `Operation` that regroups its input by a key, each group running independently and completing in any order: `group` is one.
- [`Reduction`](types/Reduction.hmd) — An `Operation` that collapses its whole input to one value through a combiner the runtime holds, so each split may be reduced and the results combined: `size` combines by addition, `contains` by disjunction.
- [`Barrier`](types/Barrier.hmd) — An `Operation` that needs its whole input before any output, so it is not split: `sort`, `take` and `reduce` are barriers.
- [`Pair`](types/Pair.hmd) — Two values held together, each at its own type: what `zip`, `enumerate`, `entries` and `join` produce, and what `Pair(first = a, second = b)` builds.
- [`Violation`](types/Violation.hmd) — One reason a value does not read as a contract, as `validate(T)` reports it.
- [`Invalid`](types/Invalid.hmd) — A value that did not read as a contract, and every reason why: its `contract`, the `value`, and the `violations`.
- [`Diagnostic`](types/Diagnostic.hmd) — A diagnostic a run delivered, read back as data: its `phase`, `kind`, `severity` and `disposition`, its `message`, and where it points, `origin` and `span`, a `{start, end}` byte range.

## Implementations

```hql
impl Eq for Bool
impl Eq for Int
impl Eq for Float
impl Eq for Decimal
impl Eq for String
impl Eq for Date
impl Eq for DateTime
impl Eq for Instant
impl Eq for Duration
impl Eq for Bytes
```

`Bool`, `Int`, `Float`, `Decimal`, `String`, the time scalars and
`Bytes` compare with `==` by value.

```hql
impl Ord for Int
impl Ord for Float
impl Ord for Decimal
impl Ord for String
impl Ord for Date
impl Ord for DateTime
impl Ord for Instant
impl Ord for Duration
impl Ord for Bytes
```

`Int`, `Float` and `Decimal` are ordered by value, `String` character by
character, `Date`, `DateTime` and `Instant` by time, and `Duration` by
length, and `Bytes` octet by octet, a shorter prefix first.

```hql
impl Add for Int
impl Add for Float
impl Add for Decimal
impl Add for String
impl Add for Duration
impl Add for Bytes
```

`+` adds `Int`s, `Float`s, `Decimal`s and `Duration`s, and concatenates
two `String`s or two `Bytes`: `"ab" + "cd"` is `"abcd"`.

```hql
impl Sub for Int
impl Sub for Float
impl Sub for Decimal
impl Sub for Duration
```

`-` subtracts `Int`s, `Float`s, `Decimal`s and `Duration`s.

```hql
impl Mul for Int
impl Mul for Float
impl Mul for Decimal
```

`*` multiplies `Int`s, `Float`s and `Decimal`s.

```hql
impl Div for Int
impl Div for Float
impl Div for Decimal
```

`/` divides `Int`s, `Float`s and `Decimal`s: `7 / 2` is `3`.

```hql
impl Rem for Int
impl Rem for Float
impl Rem for Decimal
```

`%` is the remainder of `Int`s, `Float`s and `Decimal`s: `7 % 2` is `1`.

```hql
impl Index<Data> for Data
```

`xs[i]` over `Data` is the document's own element at `i`.

```hql
impl Slice for Data
```

`xs[a:b]` over `Data` is the part of the document's sequence, as `Data`.
<!-- hql reference: end -->
